###### 4.4.1 Investigation Team

All incidents and near-misses shall be investigated. The investigation team shall be appointed by the CAB Director in consultation with the LSO and shall comprise:

a) The LSO, who shall chair the investigation;

b) A representative from OSHE;

c) The Principal Investigator or laboratory supervisor responsible for the affected area;

d) A member of the Biosafety Committee, where the incident involves regulated biological agents or where the risk assessment indicates a potential for significant harm; and

e) Any other person with relevant expertise, as determined by the CAB Director.

For minor incidents with no potential for exposure or release, the LSO may conduct the investigation alone and report the findings to the Biosafety Committee at its next scheduled meeting.

###### 4.4.2 Investigation Scope

The investigation shall establish:

a) The sequence of events leading to the incident;

b) The root cause(s), including contributing human, organisational, and technical factors;

c) The adequacy of existing risk assessments and SOPs;

d) Whether the incident indicates a systemic issue affecting other laboratories or activities; and

e) The effectiveness of the immediate response.

The investigation shall be completed within 14 working days of the incident, unless an extension is approved by the CAB Director. The CAB Director shall approve extensions only where the complexity of the investigation warrants additional time, and shall document the reasons for the extension.

